>
  <h2>Which supplier certificates should you track?</h2>
  <p>
    Which certificates you need depends on your sector, but most SMEs end up tracking a similar core set. Each one is a document of external origin under <a href="/guides/control-of-external-documents">ISO 9001 clause 7.5.3.2</a>, and most also feed clause 8.4, verifying what a supplier provides before you rely on it.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Management system certificates</strong>: your supplier's own ISO 9001 certificate — issued to either ISO 9001:2015 or ISO 9001:2026, both valid during the transition to the newer edition — or a food-safety scheme certificate such as FSSC 22000 or a <a href="https://www.brcgs.com/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">BRCGS</a> standard, benchmarked by the <a href="https://mygfsi.com/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Global Food Safety Initiative</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Religious or dietary certifications</strong>: halal and kosher certificates, where relevant to your product.</li>
    <li><strong>Certificates of analysis (COA)</strong>, confirming a specific batch or lot meets a stated specification.</li>
    <li><strong>Allergen and GMO statements</strong>, confirming what a raw material does or doesn't contain.</li>
    <li><strong>Specifications</strong> the supplier issues for their own product or material.</li>
    <li><strong>Safety data sheets (SDS)</strong> and calibration certificates for supplied equipment or instruments.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Not every certificate needs the same level of scrutiny. A management system certificate from an accredited body carries more weight than a self-declared statement, and it's reasonable to check the accreditation itself — a body like <a href="https://www.sanas.co.za/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">SANAS</a> in South Africa maintains a public register of who it accredits.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example: a food manufacturer buying from three suppliers — Northwind Ingredients for a flavour compound, Kestrel Packaging for retort pouches, and Silverleaf Botanics for a plant extract — would typically hold a certificate of analysis per batch from Northwind, a certificate of conformance for the pouch specification from Kestrel, and a combined certificate of analysis and allergen statement from Silverleaf. Three suppliers, three different document types, three different renewal rhythms — which is exactly why a single register beats trying to remember each supplier's pattern from memory.
  </p>

  <h2>How do you collect certificates from suppliers?</h2>
  <p>
    Build collection into onboarding rather than chasing it later: ask a new supplier for their current certificates before you place a first order, and set an expectation in your supplier agreement that renewed certificates are sent to you automatically, not only when you ask.
  </p>
  <p>
    In practice, most SMEs still end up chasing at least some suppliers by email as certificates approach expiry. Keep the request specific — name the certificate and the date it's due to expire — rather than a general "please send your latest paperwork," which is easy for a busy supplier contact to ignore or misread.
  </p>
  <p>
    Larger or higher-risk suppliers are worth a scheduled annual check even if nothing has visibly lapsed: ask them to confirm nothing has changed since their last certificate, since a supplier can quietly drop an accreditation, change a manufacturing site, or reformulate a product without necessarily telling every customer. Smaller or lower-risk suppliers can usually be managed on an exception basis — you only chase them when a certificate is due.
  </p>

  <h2>How do you record and check an expiry date?</h2>
  <p>
    Record four things for every certificate: the supplier and product it applies to, who issued it, the expiry or review date, and where the current version is filed. Check the date against the document itself, not against what you were told — issue dates and expiry dates on certificates are sometimes transposed or left blank, and catching that on receipt is far cheaper than catching it at audit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where a certificate has no expiry date printed on it, such as many safety data sheets, set a periodic review date instead so someone still confirms it hasn't been superseded by a newer version.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also helps to record the certificate against the specific product or batch it covers, not just the supplier generically. A single supplier can hold several certificates that apply to different products or even different manufacturing sites, and treating them as one blanket "Northwind Ingredients certificate" in your register makes it easy to miss that the certificate covering the product you actually buy has lapsed while an unrelated one is still current.
  </p>

  <h2>How do reminders work, and who should get them?</h2>
  <p>
    A reminder is only useful if it reaches someone who can act on it, with enough lead time to act. Thirty days before expiry is a common default: long enough to chase a supplier for a renewed certificate, short enough that the reminder still feels relevant when it arrives.
  </p>
  <p>
    Send the reminder to a named owner, not a shared inbox nobody checks daily, and repeat it if the certificate is still outstanding closer to the date. A single reminder sent once and forgotten is one of the most common ways a certificate lapses unnoticed.
  </p>
  <p>
    It's worth deciding in advance what a reminder is actually for: is it a prompt to chase the supplier, or a prompt to stop using the material until a renewed certificate arrives? Writing that down once, as part of your document control procedure, means the person who gets the reminder at nine o'clock on a busy Friday doesn't have to work out the right response from scratch.
  </p>

  <h2>What do you do when a certificate lapses?</h2>
  <p>
    Treat a lapsed supplier certificate as a supplier issue, not just a paperwork gap: hold or flag any stock received against that certificate, contact the supplier for a renewed version, and record what happened so it's visible at your next review. If the lapse points to a wider problem with the supplier — a certification they've let go, or a specification they've quietly changed — that's a case for a formal <strong>Supplier Deviation</strong> record, which routes an investigation and a corrective action through to sign-off. See how <a href="/product/workflow-manager">Workflow Manager</a> handles that with its built-in Supplier Deviation template.
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, if Kestrel Packaging's certificate of conformance for a pouch specification lapses and the renewed version shows a changed material, that's not a filing exercise — it's a question of whether the change affects your product, and it deserves the same investigation-and-close discipline as any other supplier deviation, not a quiet note that "the paperwork's sorted now."
  </p>

  <h2>Spreadsheet vs shared drive vs dedicated software</h2>
  <p>
    All three can technically hold the same information. What differs is how much of the checking is still a person's job, and how much the system does for you.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Approach</th>
          <th>Expiry reminders</th>
          <th>Version control</th>
          <th>Audit trail</th>
          <th>Typical failure mode</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Spreadsheet</td>
          <td>Manual — someone must open it and check dates</td>
          <td>None; the latest save overwrites the last</td>
          <td>None beyond a file-modified date</td>
          <td>Nobody checks it until an audit is booked</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Shared drive with folders</td>
          <td>None built in</td>
          <td>Whoever named the file last decides which version is current</td>
          <td>None; folder structure only shows what exists, not what changed</td>
          <td>An old certificate sits alongside the new one with no way to tell which is current</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Dedicated software</td>
          <td>Automatic, computed from the stored expiry date</td>
          <td>Version history kept when a certificate is replaced</td>
          <td>Every upload, approval and status change recorded</td>
          <td>Requires a monthly fee and a short setup step</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    A spreadsheet or shared drive is a genuinely reasonable choice while you have a handful of suppliers and one person doing the checking — see our <a href="/guides/spreadsheets-vs-qms-software">honest comparison of spreadsheets and QMS software</a> for where that stops being true. Once you have dozens of suppliers, several editors, or an audit history to defend, the manual checking step is the thing most likely to fail quietly.
  </p>
